Ladybird tries the highest-priority layer first when font faces share
the same matching descriptors. It tries the last rule in that layer
first, falling back if the face fails to load or excludes the character.
Scripts that iterate document.fonts still see the faces in source order.

Blink orders CSS-connected faces by cascade layer in FontFaceList::
Insert(). CSSSegmentedFontFace::GetFontData() visits them in reverse
through FontFaceList::ForEachReverse(). WebKit walks its family faces
backward in CSSFontFaceSet::fontFace() and sorts them stably by style
distance. Gecko appends faces in gfxUserFontFamily::AddFontEntry() and
visits them backward in gfxFontFamily::FindAllFontsForStyle().

Chrome 158.0.8127.37, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 all used the
later face for exact and nearest-weight matches, skipped an overridden
broken font and a face outside the Unicode range, and fell back after
the preferred face failed. In each browser the last rule rendered at
80px, where Ladybird had used the first rule and rendered at 23px.

Firefox and WebKit kept the later rule first after an earlier face
changed its weight and then restored it. Chrome used the earlier face.
Chrome and Firefox recomputed layer priority after the sheet that
declared the order was disabled or removed. WebKit kept the previous
priority. Ladybird follows the rule order and active cascade layers.

All three browsers also honored the layer order in adoptedStyleSheets.
Replacing the sheet that declared the order changed the chosen face
from the 80px sample to the 23px sample in each browser.
